/*!
  \page ponca_getting_started_page Getting started

  \tableofcontents

  \section getting_started_requirements_sec Requirements

  %Ponca is a header-only C++ library. Using it requires:
  - a C++20 compiler. The continuous integration tests GCC (Linux), Clang (macOS) and MSVC (Windows),
  - <a href="https://eigen.tuxfamily.org/" target="_blank">Eigen</a> 3.4 or later, the swiss army knife of linear
    algebra. When building with CMake, Eigen is searched with `find_package(Eigen3 3.4)`; if it is not found, the git
    submodule `external/eigen` is used,
  - CMake 3.12 or later, to use the CMake integration or to build the examples, the tests and this documentation.

  Optional features have additional requirements:

  | Feature                                        | Requirements |
  |------------------------------------------------|--------------|
  | CUDA kernels                                   | CUDA 12 or later (C++20 support), see \ref ponca_gpu_page |
  | SYCL kernels                                   | a SYCL implementation (the examples are built with AdaptiveCpp) |
  | Documentation                                  | Doxygen, Graphviz (`dot`), BibTeX |
  | Examples                                       | OpenMP (optional); PCL for \ref example_cxx_pcl_page; libpng for \ref example_cu_ssc_page; PyCUDA and matplotlib for \ref example_python_ssc_page |
  | Python binding                                 | Python 3.9 or later with its development files, a C++ compiler and CMake, see \ref pyponca |

  \section getting_started_download_sec Download

  Releases are available at https://github.com/poncateam/ponca/releases/latest/. The development version is
  available on the `master` branch of the repository:
  \code{.sh}
  git clone https://github.com/poncateam/ponca.git
  \endcode

  The library is being refactored towards version 2.0, which introduces breaking changes in the API:
  - tag `v1.4` is the last release following the previous API,
  - tags `v2.0.alphaX` follow the new API, described in this documentation. Each alpha release is tested, but may
    introduce API changes. They are recommended for new projects,
  - the head of the `master` branch follows the development of the library.

  \section getting_started_installation_sec Installation

  %Ponca is header-only: using it only requires to include its headers, and to compile with C++20 and Eigen in the
  include path.

  | Header                          | Content |
  |---------------------------------|---------|
  | `<Ponca/Ponca>`                 | all the modules |
  | `<Ponca/Fitting>`               | \ref fitting (includes the Common module) |
  | `<Ponca/SpatialPartitioning>`   | \ref spatialpartitioning |
  | `<Ponca/Common>`                | \ref common |

  For instance, without build system:
  \code{.sh}
  g++ -std=c++20 -O2 -I path/to/ponca -I path/to/eigen main.cpp -o main
  \endcode

  The CMake project of %Ponca defines the targets `Ponca::Common`, `Ponca::Fitting`, `Ponca::SpatialPartitioning` and
  `Ponca::Instantiate` (see \ref getting_started_installation_compilation_instantiate). Linking a target adds the
  include directories and Eigen to the dependent target.

  \subsection getting_started_installation_cmakesubdirectory_subsec Use as CMake subdirectory

  %Ponca can be included in an existing CMake project with `add_subdirectory`, as demonstrated in
  https://github.com/poncateam/ci-submodule-helper, where %Ponca is a git submodule of the project:
  \code{.cmake}
  find_package(Eigen3 QUIET)

  add_subdirectory(ponca)

  add_executable(test main.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(test Ponca::Fitting)
  \endcode

  \subsection getting_started_installation_cmake_subsec CMake package

  The `install` target generates a relocatable CMake package (in `CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX`), which is used as follows:
  \code{.cmake}
  cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.12)

  project(PoncaTest LANGUAGES CXX)

  find_package(Ponca REQUIRED)

  add_executable(test main.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(test Ponca::Fitting)
  \endcode

  \subsection getting_started_installation_cmake_cpm CPM

  <a href="https://github.com/cpm-cmake/CPM.cmake" target="_blank">CPM</a> is a CMake package manager that downloads,
  builds and manages dependencies with a single line of CMake. %Ponca is compatible with CPM. Once the
  <a href="https://github.com/cpm-cmake/CPM.cmake/blob/master/cmake/CPM.cmake" target="_blank">CPM.cmake</a> file is
  downloaded, the following script gets you started:
  \code{.cmake}
  cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.12)
  project(PoncaTest LANGUAGES CXX)

  include(CPM.cmake) # Or any path to the downloaded file
  CPMAddPackage("gh:poncateam/ponca#master") # Downloads Ponca and runs its CMake script

  add_executable(test main.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(test PRIVATE Ponca::Fitting) # Or any other Ponca target
  \endcode

  Other branches, tags or commits, and options, can be given to `CPMAddPackage`, see the
  <a href="https://github.com/cpm-cmake/CPM.cmake" target="_blank">CPM documentation</a>.

  \section getting_started_first_program_sec A first program

  The following program estimates the normal of a point cloud at one of its points, by fitting a plane to its
  neighbors (see \ref fitting for the explanations):
  \code
  #include <Ponca/Ponca>
  #include <iostream>
  #include <vector>

  using Point  = Ponca::PointPosition<double, 3>;
  using Filter = Ponca::DistWeightFilter<Point, Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel<double>>;
  using Fit    = Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, Ponca::CovariancePlaneFit>;

  int main()
  {
      // Sample a sphere of radius 1
      std::vector<Point> points(10000);
      for (auto& point : points)
          point = Point(Point::VectorType::Random().normalized());

      // Build a kd-tree to collect the neighborhoods
      Ponca::KdTreeDense<Point> tree(points);

      // Fit a plane to the neighbors of the first point, in a ball of radius 0.1
      const double radius        = 0.1;
      const Point::VectorType& p = tree.points()[0].pos();

      Fit fit;
      fit.setNeighborFilter({p, radius});
      if (fit.computeWithIds(tree.rangeNeighbors(p, radius), tree.points()) == Ponca::STABLE)
          std::cout << "Normal at " << p.transpose() << ": " << fit.primitiveGradient().transpose() << std::endl;
  }
  \endcode

  With CMake, assuming that %Ponca has been cloned in the folder `ponca` next to `main.cpp`:
  \code{.cmake}
  cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.12)
  project(PoncaFirstProgram LANGUAGES CXX)
  set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)

  add_subdirectory(ponca) # or: find_package(Ponca REQUIRED)

  add_executable(normals main.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(normals PRIVATE Ponca::Fitting Ponca::SpatialPartitioning)
  \endcode

  \subsection getting_started_installation_compilation_subsec Building the examples, the tests and the documentation

  The examples, the tests and the documentation are built with CMake:
  \code{.sh}
  cmake -S . -B build # Creates the build folder and configures the project
  \endcode

  The available options are:
  \dontinclude CMakeLists.txt
  \skip USER OPTIONS
  \until END USER OPTIONS

  Then build a target with
  \code{.sh}
  cmake --build build --target TARGET
  \endcode
  where `TARGET` is:
   - `ponca-doc`: generates this documentation with Doxygen in `build/doc/html` (requires `PONCA_CONFIGURE_DOC`),
   - `ponca-examples`: builds all the examples (requires `PONCA_CONFIGURE_EXAMPLES`); each example also has its own
     target, given in its page (see \ref example_page),
   - `buildtests`: builds the tests (requires `PONCA_CONFIGURE_TESTS`),
   - `test`: runs the tests (`ctest` can also be used directly, from the build folder),
   - `install`: generates the relocatable CMake package and installs it in `CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX`, along with the
     documentation if it has been built.

  \subsection getting_started_installation_compilation_instantiate Reducing compile time

  The extensive use of templates increases compilation times. To alleviate this burden, %Ponca provides a shared
  library, `Ponca::Instantiate`, that pre-instantiates the most common classes of the library. Linking it is the only
  step required:
  \code{.cmake}
  target_link_libraries(your_target PUBLIC Ponca::Instantiate)
  \endcode

  The library is built the first time it is required, which can take a while. Linking it defines the macro
  `_PONCA_INSTANTIATE_LIBRARY`, so that `<Ponca/Ponca>` declares the pre-instantiated classes as `extern`: code using
  exactly these types reuses them instead of instantiating them again.

  \note Pre-instantiation is disabled with MSVC (Windows default compiler), because MSVC compiles all the member
  functions of explicitly instantiated classes, including those that are not valid for every dimension. With MSVC,
  `Ponca::Instantiate` is an empty interface library, so the CMake code remains valid.

  \subsubsection getting_started_installation_compilation_instantiate_params Instantiation parameters

  The instantiated classes are the baskets listed in `Ponca/src/Common/instantiate/instantiate_list/baskets.h` (and
  `basketsdiff.h` for derivatives), for every combination of the selected scalar types, dimensions, point types,
  neighbor filters and derivative types. The selection is controlled by CMake options:

  | Option                                 | Default | Effect |
  |----------------------------------------|---------|--------|
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_DEFAULT`            | `ON`    | selects `float`, 3D, Ponca::PointPositionNormal and Ponca::DistWeightFilter with Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel (no derivatives) |
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_ALL`                | `OFF`   | selects everything (takes priority over the other options) |
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_FLOAT`, `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_DOUBLE` | `OFF` | adds a scalar type |
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_2D`, `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_3D` | `OFF` | adds a dimension |
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_POINTPOSITION`, `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_POINTPOSITIONNORMAL` | `OFF` | adds a point type |
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_SMOOTHWEIGHT`, `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_CONSTANTWEIGHT`, `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_NOWEIGHT` | `OFF` | adds a neighbor filter |
  | `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_SPACEDER`, `PONCA_INSTANTIATE_SCALESPACEDER` | `OFF` | adds the baskets with space (or scale and space) derivatives |

  Every option adds an item to its category, and each item roughly doubles the compilation time of the library. At
  least one scalar type, one dimension, one point type and one filter must be selected for anything to be
  instantiated. For instance, to only instantiate double precision baskets for 3D oriented points:
  \code{.sh}
  cmake -S . -B build -DPONCA_INSTANTIATE_DEFAULT=OFF -DPONCA_INSTANTIATE_DOUBLE=ON -DPONCA_INSTANTIATE_3D=ON \
        -DPONCA_INSTANTIATE_POINTPOSITIONNORMAL=ON -DPONCA_INSTANTIATE_SMOOTHWEIGHT=ON
  \endcode

  \section getting_started_first_step_sec Next steps

  You are now ready to play with %Ponca! The \ref user_manual_page describes the library, and the \ref example_page
  show complete programs.

  If you get stuck or find a bug, please open an issue on https://github.com/poncateam/ponca/issues: the library is
  under active development, and we are pleased to get your feedback.
 */
